    <title>Hydraulic Erosion Models Comparison</title>
    <briefdescription>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para><anchor id="md_docs_2content_2dev_2hydraulic__comparison_1autotoc_md16"/> This document provides a comparative analysis of the three primary hydraulic erosion approaches implemented in HighMap:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>**<computeroutput>hydraulic_particle</computeroutput>**: Lagrangian discrete droplet simulation.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>**<computeroutput>hydraulic_stream_log</computeroutput>**: Eulerian $D_\infty$ logarithmic stream power incision.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>**<computeroutput>hydraulic_mise</computeroutput>**: Multiscale Implicit Stream-power Erosion with coupled sediment dynamics.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para>The visualization and benchmarks below are produced by <ulink url="file:///home/glop/Documents/dev/HighMap/examples/ex_hydraulic_comparison/ex_hydraulic_comparison.cpp"><computeroutput>ex_hydraulic_comparison.cpp</computeroutput></ulink>.</para>
<sect1 id="md_docs_2content_2dev_2hydraulic__comparison_1autotoc_md17">
<title>1. Visual Comparison</title>
<para>The following banner shows the original un-eroded conical terrain alongside the result of each solver applied to the same input heightfield:</para>
<para><image type="html" name="../../images/hydraulic_comparison_terrains.png" inline="yes"></image>
</para>
<para><emphasis>Left to right: (1) Original un-eroded terrain, (2) <computeroutput>hydraulic_particle</computeroutput>, (3) <computeroutput>hydraulic_stream_log</computeroutput>, (4) <computeroutput>hydraulic_mise</computeroutput>.</emphasis></para>
<sect2 id="md_docs_2content_2dev_2hydraulic__comparison_1autotoc_md18">
<title>Erosion Difference Maps</title>
<para>The net erosion maps ($z_{\text{before}} - z \ge 0$) highlight the spatial distribution and channelization patterns of each model:</para>
<para><image type="html" name="../../images/hydraulic_comparison_erosion_maps.png" inline="yes"></image>
</para>
<para><emphasis>Left to right: (1) <computeroutput>hydraulic_particle</computeroutput> erosion delta, (2) <computeroutput>hydraulic_stream_log</computeroutput> erosion delta, (3) <computeroutput>hydraulic_mise</computeroutput> erosion delta.</emphasis></para>
</sect2>
</sect1>
<sect1 id="md_docs_2content_2dev_2hydraulic__comparison_1autotoc_md19">
<title>2. Qualitative Characteristics</title>
<para><table rows="8" cols="4"><row>
<entry thead="yes" align='left'><para>Feature   </para>
</entry><entry thead="yes" align='left'><para><computeroutput>hydraulic_particle</computeroutput>   </para>
</entry><entry thead="yes" align='left'><para><computeroutput>hydraulic_stream_log</computeroutput>   </para>
</entry><entry thead="yes" align='left'><para><computeroutput>hydraulic_mise</computeroutput>    </para>
</entry></row>
<row>
<entry thead="no" align='left'><para><bold>Model Type</bold>   </para>
</entry><entry thead="no" align='left'><para>Lagrangian (discrete droplets)   </para>
</entry><entry thead="no" align='left'><para>Eulerian (raster flow accumulation)   </para>
</entry><entry thead="no" align='left'><para>Topological drainage graph &amp; implicit PDE    </para>
